{"name":"Sensitive Scorpion","hex":"#cc2266","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sensitive","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the faculty of sensation; pertaining to the senses.","Responsive to stimuli."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person with a paranormal sensitivity to something that most cannot perceive."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sensitive"},{"term":"scorpion","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the order Scorpiones of arachnids related to spiders, characterised by two large front pincers and a curved tail with a venomous sting at the end.","An ancient military engine for hurling stones and other missiles."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/scorpion"}],"entries":[{"title":"Scorpion","blocks":[{"text":"Scorpions are predatory arachnids with eight legs, a pair of grasping pincers and a narrow, segmented tail, often carried in a characteristic forward curve over the back and always ending with a stinger. The evolutionary history of scorpions goes back to the Silurian Period, approximately 430 million years ago. They mainly live in deserts but have adapted to a wide range of environmental conditions, and can be found on all continents except Antarctica. There are over 2,500 described species, with 22 extant (living) families recognized to date. Their taxonomy is being revised to account for 21st-century genomic studies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Scorpion_Photograph_By_Shantanu_Kuveskar.jpg/330px-Scorpion_Photograph_By_Shantanu_Kuveskar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scorpion"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Scorpiones_distribution.png/500px-Scorpiones_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/